<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="u4a-ct-labeled">
  <title>Unit 4a Subgoal Labeled Puzzles for Toggle Code</title>
  <p>
    These are copies of the Arrays Mixed Up Code Practice puzzles with subgoal
    labels added. The Arrays Toggle Code Practice page uses them as the Get
    Help puzzles for its write code problems. Each loop header and its opening
    brace are one block, so one label sits on each loop, and each if statement
    is one block with its body.
  </p>

  <activity label="arrayex1muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex1muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment should loop from 0 to less than the length
        of the array and double each element in the array <c>arr</c> and also
        print out each new value on the same line separated by “, “. The
        finished code should print “2, 4, 6, 8, 10, “. But, the blocks have been
        mixed up and include extra blocks that are not needed in a correct
        solution. Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the correct
        order on the right. Click the Check button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="4">
        <cline>// Store the numbers in the array</cline>
        <cline>int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>// Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>{</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>// Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>for (int i = 0; i &lt;= arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>{</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Set the element at this index to twice its value</cline>
          <cline>    arr[i] = arr[i] * 2;</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Set the element at this index to twice its value</cline>
          <cline>    arr[i] = i * 2;</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Print the doubled element without moving to a new line</cline>
          <cline>    System.out.print(arr[i] + ", ");</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Print the doubled element without moving to a new line</cline>
          <cline>    System.out.println(arr[i] + ", ");</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <cline>} // end for loop</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex2muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex2muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment should fill an array with elements that
        count up from 0 to 50 by 5 (0, 5, 10, 15, 20…) and print out each
        element on a separate line. But the blocks have been mixed up and
        include extra blocks that are not needed in a correct solution. Drag the
        needed blocks from the left and put them in the correct order on the
        right. Click the Check button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="2">
        <cline>// Create the array to hold the values from 0 to 50</cline>
        <cline>int[] arr = new int[11];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>// Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 11; i++)</cline>
          <cline>{</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>// Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 11; i++)</cline>
          <cline>{</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Set the element at this index to the multiple of 5 for that index</cline>
          <cline>    arr[i] = i * 5;</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Set the element at this index to the multiple of 5 for that index</cline>
          <cline>    arr[i] = i + 5;</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Print the element on its own line</cline>
          <cline>    System.out.println(arr[i]);</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Print the element on its own line</cline>
          <cline>    System.out.println(i);</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <cline>} // end for loop</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex3muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex3muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment should print each element in the array
        that is even using an enhanced for each loop. But, the blocks have been
        mixed up and include extra blocks that are not needed in a correct
        solution. Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the correct
        order on the right. Click the Check button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>// Store the numbers in the array</cline>
        <cline>int[] arr = {14, -5, 2, 17, 29, -8, 36};</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>// Use an enhanced for-each loop to visit each element of the array</cline>
          <cline>for (int value : arr)</cline>
          <cline>{</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>// Use an enhanced for-each loop to visit each element of the array</cline>
          <cline>for (value : arr)</cline>
          <cline>{</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Check for an even element and print it</cline>
          <cline>    if (value % 2 == 0)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
          <cline>        System.out.println(value);</cline>
          <cline>    } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Check for an even element and print it</cline>
          <cline>    if (value / 2 == 0)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
          <cline>        System.out.println(value);</cline>
          <cline>    } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Check for an even element and print it</cline>
          <cline>    if (arr[value] % 2 == 0)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
          <cline>        System.out.println(arr[value]);</cline>
          <cline>    } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <cline>} // end for loop</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex4muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex4muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment is a method that should return the
        smallest integer given an array of integers (the parameter). But, the
        blocks have been mixed up and include extra blocks that are not needed
        in a correct solution. Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the
        correct order with the correct indentation on the right. Click the Check
        button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="2">
        <cline>public static int findSmallest(int[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <cline>    // Start with the first element as the smallest so far</cline>
        <cline>    int smallest = arr[0];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt;= arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        // Replace the smallest so far when you find a smaller element</cline>
          <cline>        if (arr[i] &lt; smallest)</cline>
          <cline>        {</cline>
          <cline>            smallest = arr[i];</cline>
          <cline>        } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>        // Replace the smallest so far when you find a smaller element</cline>
          <cline>        if (arr[i] &gt; smallest)</cline>
          <cline>        {</cline>
          <cline>            smallest = arr[i];</cline>
          <cline>        } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="7">
        <cline>    } // end for loop</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <cline>    // Return the smallest integer after checking every element</cline>
        <cline>    return smallest;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <cline>} // end findSmallest method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex5muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex5muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment is a method that should return the average
        given an array of integers (the parameter). But, the blocks have been
        mixed up and include extra blocks that are not needed in a correct
        solution. Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the correct
        order with the correct indentation on the right. Click the Check button
        to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="1">
        <cline>public static double findAverage(int[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <cline>    // Start the running total at zero</cline>
        <cline>    double sum = 0;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 1; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        // Add the element at this index to the running total</cline>
          <cline>        sum += arr[i];</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>        // Add the element at this index to the running total</cline>
          <cline>        sum = arr[i];</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="7">
        <cline>    } // end for loop</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <cline>    // Turn the running total into the average and return it</cline>
        <cline>    return (sum / arr.length);</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <cline>} // end findAverage method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex6muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex6muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment is a method that should return the largest
        integer given an array of integers (the parameter). But, the blocks have
        been mixed up and include extra blocks that are not needed in a correct
        solution. Drag the needed code from the left to the right and put them
        in order with the correct indention so that the code would work
        correctly. Click the Check button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="5">
        <cline>public static int findLargest(int[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Start with the first element as the largest so far</cline>
          <cline>    int largest = arr[0];</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Start with the first element as the largest so far</cline>
          <cline>    int largest = arr.get(0);</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Start with the first element as the largest so far</cline>
          <cline>    int largest = arr[1];</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index of the array in order</cline>
        <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
        <cline>    {</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="7">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        // Replace the largest so far when you find a larger element</cline>
          <cline>        if (largest &lt; arr[i])</cline>
          <cline>        {</cline>
          <cline>            largest = arr[i];</cline>
          <cline>        } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>        // Replace the largest so far when you find a larger element</cline>
          <cline>        if (largest &gt; arr[i])</cline>
          <cline>        {</cline>
          <cline>            largest = arr[i];</cline>
          <cline>        } // end if</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <cline>    } // end for loop</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <cline>    // Return the largest integer after checking every element</cline>
        <cline>    return largest;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <cline>} // end findLargest method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex7muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex7muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment is a method that should return an integer
        array that is “right shifted” by one – so {6, 2, 5, 3} returns {3, 6, 2,
        5} (the parameter). Note that the method return type is int[] which
        means it will return an int array. But, the blocks have been mixed up
        and include extra blocks that are not needed in a correct solution.
        Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the correct order on the
        right. Click the Check button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="7">
        <cline>public static int[] shiftRight(int[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <cline>    // Create the result array the same length as the given array</cline>
        <cline>    int[] result = new int[arr.length];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Put the given array's end element at the front of the result</cline>
          <cline>    result[0] = arr[arr.length-1];</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Put the given array's end element at the front of the result</cline>
          <cline>    result[0] = arr[arr.length];</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to fill the remaining places in the result</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length - 1; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to fill the remaining places in the result</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        // Put the element one place to the right in the result</cline>
          <cline>        result[i + 1] = arr[i];</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>        // Put the element one place to the right in the result</cline>
          <cline>        result[i] = arr[i + 1];</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <cline>    } // end for loop</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="8">
        <cline>    // Return the shifted array</cline>
        <cline>    return result;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <cline>} // end shiftRight method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex8muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex8muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment is a method that should return a new array
        of length 2 containing the middle two elements of a given array of
        integers of even length (the parameter) – so {1,2,3,4} should return
        {2,3}. But, the blocks have been mixed up and include extra blocks
        that are not needed in a correct solution. Drag the blocks from the left
        and put them in the correct order on the right. Click the Check button
        to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>public static int[] makeMiddle(int[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <cline>    // Create the result array of length 2</cline>
        <cline>    int[] result = new int[2];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Find the index of the first of the middle two elements</cline>
          <cline>    int middleIndex = (arr.length / 2) - 1;</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Find the index of the first of the middle two elements</cline>
          <cline>    int middleIndex = (arr.length / 2);</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <cline>    // Put the middle two elements into the result</cline>
        <cline>    result[0] = arr[middleIndex];</cline>
        <cline>    result[1] = arr[middleIndex + 1];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <cline>    // Return the array of the middle two elements</cline>
        <cline>    return result;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <cline>} // end makeMiddle method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex9muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex9muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program segment is a method that should return string
        array that is in reverse order – so {“b”, “a”, “z”} should return {“z”,
        “a”, “b”}. But, the blocks have been mixed up and include extra
        blocks that are not needed in a correct solution. Drag the blocks from
        the left and put them in the correct order on the right. Click the Check
        button to check your solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="8">
        <cline>public static String[] reverse(String[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <cline>    // Create the result array the same length as the given array</cline>
        <cline>    String[] result = new String[arr.length];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="5">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Start the counter at the end position of the result array</cline>
          <cline>    int i = arr.length - 1;</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Start the counter at the end position of the result array</cline>
          <cline>    int i = arr.length;</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="7">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Use an enhanced for-each loop to visit each element of the given array</cline>
          <cline>    for (String element: arr)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Use an enhanced for-each loop to visit each element of the given array</cline>
          <cline>    for (element: arr)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <cline>        // Put the element at the counter's position in the result</cline>
        <cline>        result[i] = element;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="9">
        <cline>        // Subtract one from the counter so it moves toward the front</cline>
        <cline>        i--;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <cline>    } // end for loop</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <cline>    // Return the reversed array</cline>
        <cline>    return result;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <cline>} // end reverse method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

  <activity label="arrayex10muc-sgl" xml:id="arrayex10muc-sgl" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="java">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following program copies the first half of an array given as an
        argument to the method into a result array which is returned. But, the
        blocks have been mixed up and include extra blocks that are not needed
        in a correct solution. Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the
        correct order on the right. Click the Check button to check your
        solution.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="5">
        <cline>public static int[] firstHalf(int[] arr)</cline>
        <cline>{</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="1">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Create the result array half the length of the given array</cline>
          <cline>    int[] result = new int[arr.length / 2];</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Create the result array half the length of the given array</cline>
          <cline>    int[] result = new int[(arr.length + 1) / 2];</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="6">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index in the first half</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; result.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    // Use a for loop to visit each index in the first half</cline>
          <cline>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</cline>
          <cline>    {</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>

      <block order="2">
        <cline>        // Put the given array's element at this index into the result</cline>
        <cline>        result[i] = arr[i];</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="4">
        <cline>    } // end for loop</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="7">
        <cline>    // Return the array holding the first half</cline>
        <cline>    return result;</cline>
      </block>

      <block order="3">
        <cline>} // end firstHalf method</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
</section>
